# ankitools

Scripts and files for my Anki study of Mandarin. Some large dictionary
sources are excluded from git repo but noted in dict/README.

## multizhdict

Segments a string of Chinese characters into words and prints every
definition the dictionaries have for each, Chinese before English:

    ./multizhdict 我愛北京天安門
    echo 我愛北京天安門 | ./multizhdict

`multizhdict` is a wrapper that pipes `gomultizhdict` into `less -S`, which
truncates long lines rather than wrapping them so that the columns stay
lined up; scroll sideways with the arrow keys. Run `gomultizhdict` directly
to send the output anywhere else. Input may be traditional or simplified:
both headword forms of every entry are matched.

### Building

    make                 # builds bin/gomultizhdict and bin/gozhread
    make test
    make install         # PREFIX=/usr/local

Go 1.24 or later. multizhdict uses nothing outside the standard library;
zhread uses tcell for the terminal, which `make` fetches through the Go
module proxy on the first build.

The binary finds the dictionaries by looking for a `dict` directory at or
above its own location, and then at or above the working directory. An
installed copy sits outside this checkout and so finds neither: point
`MULTIZHDICT_DICT` at the absolute path of `dict/`, or pass `-dict`.

### Output

One line per definition, separated from the next word by a rule as wide as
the terminal. Every line carries the same four fields in the same order —
traditional, simplified, pinyin, definition — padded into columns by
display width, with blanks where a dictionary has nothing for a field.
`-tsv` emits single tabs instead, for feeding something else.

Each language has a main dictionary and a fallback, the same as zhread's
defaults: the fallback's entries are printed only for a word the main
dictionary does not define, where for CC-CEDICT an entry that only points
elsewhere ("variant of …", "see …[…]", a bare classifier) does not count.
Every entry the dictionaries used have for the word is printed, which is
how the other readings of a character like 行 show up. The Chinese lines
come first, then the English ones, and each language is ordered by
decreasing definition width on its own: sorting the whole group at once
would interleave the two, because a long English gloss outruns a short
Chinese one. A word only the Chinese dictionaries carry comes out as
Chinese lines alone.

An English definition that only points elsewhere ("see …", "CL:…") comes
after the rest. An entry that is only a variant of another the word has,
with the same reading, is left out: CC-CEDICT's 煖, 㬉 and 煗 simplify to 暖
and say "variant of 暖[nuǎn]", and 兩岸詞典's 偺 is 我。同「咱」。. A word
written only in a variant form, such as 煖, gets the standard form's
entries instead, since they are where its definitions are.

- Chinese: dict/csld.tsv (兩岸詞典), falling back to
  dict/MoEDICT/MoEDict-05-Simp03-cards.txt and dict/ccdict_additions.tsv.
- English: dict/cedict_parsed.tsv and dict/cedict_additions.tsv, falling
  back to dict/wikt.tsv (Wiktionary).

A 兩岸詞典 or Wiktionary definition's parts are joined by ｜ on its one
line, as MoEDict's sections are, and 兩岸詞典's marks for a word or sense
used only on the Mainland (★) or in Taiwan (▲) are spelled out as 〈大陸〉
and 〈臺灣〉. Nothing in the output says which source a line came from.
Definitions of equal width keep load order: the dictionary before its
additions, and within one file the order the entries appear in it.

A word that no source has gets one line instead, `字典查無「X」`, flush left
rather than in the columns. It is the only line that repeats the input,
which is what makes such a word identifiable at all: with no entry it fills
no column.

### Taiwan and Mainland readings

MoEDict gives Taiwan readings and CC-CEDICT Mainland ones; 兩岸詞典 and
Wiktionary give both. Where the two differ, the Mainland reading is
printed first and the Taiwan one noted after it, on every line for the
word whichever dictionary it came from:

    企業  企业  qǐ yè (TW qì yè)  從事生產、運輸、貿易等經濟活動…
    企業  企业  qǐ yè (TW qì yè)  company; firm; enterprise; …

`-pron tw` prints the Taiwan reading first instead: `qì yè (CN qǐ yè)`.

The differences come from the cross-strait dictionary (兩岸詞典), which
gives both readings for every entry where they differ; dict/csld_pron.tsv
holds the 6,443 of its rows that differ in pronunciation rather than only
in notation (see dict/README). For words it lacks, dict/wikt_pron.tsv adds
2,269 pairs from Wiktionary, such as 導播 dǎo bò against dǎo bō. A
reading is looked up in the column for the side its dictionary speaks for,
and in the other column too for MoEDict, which sometimes already gives the
Mainland reading (認識 rèn shi). CC-CEDICT's own "Taiwan pr. [...]" note,
when it ends a definition, moves into the reading column; one inside
parentheses applies to a single sense and stays where it is.

The table's rows are per sense but do not say which sense, so a row is not
applied where it could belong to another entry. A Taiwan reading that
CC-CEDICT also gives the word is left alone: MoEDict's 模 mó "model" is
mó on the Mainland too, and the table's 模 mó → mú is about 模 "mould".
Likewise a Mainland reading is not matched when the dictionary has the
row's other reading as an entry of its own: 和 hé "sum" is not the 和 hàn
"and" that the Mainland reads hé. Such entries print the reading their
dictionary gives, unmarked.

A word neither table has falls back on the two dictionaries themselves:
where MoEDict and CC-CEDICT each give it exactly one reading and the two
differ, MoEDict's is taken for Taiwan's and CC-CEDICT's for the
Mainland's, on both lines (一字不識 yī zì bù shí (TW yī zì bù shì)). This
applies to words of two or more characters that 兩岸詞典 does not have,
about 700 of them. Neutral tones count (保住 bǎo zhù (TW bǎo zhu)). About
one pair in twenty-five, judged from a sample of 80, is two senses rather
than two standards: 下咽 xià yàn "to swallow" against xià yān
"hypopharynx". Single characters are left out because that is much
commoner among them (羧 zuī a woollen cloth, suō the carboxyl), and so
are words with several readings in either dictionary. Any other word
prints the reading its dictionary gives, unmarked.

### Segmentation

| -seg    | segmentation                                                     |
| ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------- |
| viterbi | best-scoring path under a unigram word-frequency model (default) |
| fmm     | forward maximum matching: longest word from the left             |
| bmm     | backward maximum matching: longest word from the right           |
| bidi    | both maximum matches, then the tie-break below                   |

Maximum matching takes the longest dictionary word starting at the current
position, then resumes after it, falling back to a single character where no
longer word is known. Run forward it errs in a predictable direction:
wherever a long word and a shorter one share a prefix it takes the long one,
so 研究生命起源 comes out as 研究生 / 命 / 起源. Run backward it makes a
different set of mistakes, and the two agree except where the run is
genuinely ambiguous. `bidi` runs both and prefers the result with fewer
words, then the one with fewer single-character words, then the backward
one.

Where both of those criteria tie, `bidi` is choosing arbitrarily, and
arbitrarily is sometimes wrong. 交流感 is 交流 / 感 forwards and 交 / 流感
backwards: two words either way, one single character either way, so the
backward reading wins only because it is the fallback, even though 流感 is a
much less common word than 交流.

`viterbi` scores the candidates instead. Each word is given the probability
count/total from dict/jieba/dict.txt, a segmentation scores the product over
its words, and the best path through the lattice of dictionary words is
taken. Every word carries a factor of 1/total, so the model penalises using
more words on its own — bidi's first criterion arrived at from the counts
rather than asserted — and it reads 交流感 as 交流 / 感, 交流 being some 40
times commoner than 流感.

Only jieba's counts are per word. Unihan's kFrequency and kHanyuPinlu, in
dict/Unihan/, are per character, and no per-character score can separate two
segmentations of one run: both readings of 交流感 are made of 交, 流 and 感,
so any score summed over the characters is the same for the two. A
dictionary word the table does not list, which is most of what the additions
files carry, scores as one occurrence — rarer than anything listed but still
a word, so 露鋒方筆館閣體 stays 露鋒 / 方筆 / 館閣體 rather than coming apart
into characters. Reading the table costs about 17 ms a query here, taking 39
ms to 56 ms, the file being 4.9 MB against the 49 MB of definition sources.

`viterbi` is the only mode that reads dict/jieba/dict.txt, and it fails with
an error if the file is missing; the other three never open it.

Runs are segmented between non-Chinese characters, which are dropped. A
full-width comma and an interpunct are exceptions, because 1,469 entries
have a comma in a headword (couplets and proverbs) and 197 have an
interpunct (transliterated names); both are skipped when no word covers
them.

### Other scripts

`add_words_ccdict.py` builds Anki rows for a list of words, prompting for
anything the dictionaries lack. `scripts/` holds one-off column fixes.

## zhread

A reader for Chinese text files that makes Anki cards of the words you
select, each with the sentence it came from:

    zhread book.txt

`zhread` is a wrapper that opens `gozhread` in its own st window, in the
font multizhdict's window uses (Liberation Mono, 24 pixels; `ZHREAD_FONT`
to change), and returns to the shell at once. If gozhread fails, the window
stays open until Enter so the error can be read. Without st or a display,
or with `ZHREAD_WINDOW=0`, it runs gozhread in the current terminal.

The words of the text come from the same segmenter as multizhdict, run in
the background a screenful at a time, and the definitions of the word under
the cursor (or of the selection) are shown below the text, each starting
on a line with the word and its reading, and every line of it indented to
where its first line starts. They are Chinese only until `e` adds the
English, except for a word with no Chinese definition, whose English is
shown at once.

Each language has a main dictionary and a fallback. Chinese comes from
兩岸詞典 (the cross-strait dictionary), whose labels 〈書〉 (literary) and
〈口〉 (colloquial) are kept, and whose marks for a word or sense used only
on the Mainland (★) or in Taiwan (▲) are spelled out as 〈大陸〉 and
〈臺灣〉; MoEDict defines the words 兩岸詞典 lacks, and `c` adds its
definitions of the rest. English comes from CC-CEDICT; Wiktionary defines
the words CC-CEDICT lacks or only points elsewhere for ("variant of …",
"see …"), and `E` adds its definitions of the rest. A fallback's
definition shown beside the main one is labelled with its name, under the
word. See dict/README for the files.

A word is shown in traditional, with the simplified form after it in brackets
when it differs and the characters the two share as ～: 企業（～业）. An entry
that is only a variant of another of the word's is left out, as multizhdict
leaves it out; when the text has the variant form, as 煖 for 暖, the standard
form's entries are shown with the variant after the word, as "暖 (煖)". Which
form the text has is judged from the words around it: the script that more of
them match only one of.

Each sense gets a line, with its examples and synonym lists after it on the
same line, in colours of their own. A part of speech starts the first sense
under it, and the rest line up after it; a sense with sub-senses, like
MoEDict's ① 結構助詞： over ⑴ to ⑷, has a line of its own, with the sub-senses
indented under it. Quotations from books are hidden until `x`, and then shown
under the sense. A sense already on a card ends with "(carded)", after a space
in English, matched against the definitions saved in cards.tsv; a carded word
none of whose senses shown matches, as one carded from the other dictionary,
has "(carded)" under the word instead. The definitions pane is as tall as what
it shows, up to half the screen, so short entries leave the text more room; it
is measured again on every redraw, a resize included. A longer entry scrolls
with `J` and `K`, and the rule above it notes how many rows are out of view (↑2
↓5), and before that which of `x`, `c`, `e` and `E` would change what the pane
shows for the word: `x` when a definition shown has quotations it hides, `c`
when MoEDict has a definition beside 兩岸詞典's, `e` when there are English
definitions after Chinese ones, `E` when Wiktionary has one beside CC-CEDICT's
while English is shown. `gh` or F1 shows the keys there instead.

Readings are Mainland, as in multizhdict; `t` adds the Taiwan reading where it
differs, as "qǐ yè (TW qì yè)", and a card made while it is shown carries both.
`-pron tw` reverses the two. Words already carded are coloured. The cursor is
white, and the rest of the word under it blue, except in visual mode, where
only the selection is coloured. Its line stays a quarter of the way down the
screen, except near the start of the file, so the text does not shift as the
definitions pane changes height.

The colours are vis's zenburn theme (lua/themes/zenburn.lua). They are all
xterm 256-colour palette entries, so st shows them exactly without true
colour.

| keys            | action                                         |
| --------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| `h j k l`       | character left, line down, line up, right      |
| `w b`           | next word, previous word                       |
| `0 $`, `g0 g$`  | start, end of the line; of the screen row      |
| `gj gk`, arrows | screen row down, up                            |
| `gg G`          | first line, last line (or line N with a count) |
| `^D ^U ^F ^B`   | half page down, up; page down, up              |
| `/ ?`, `n N`    | search forward, back (a Go regexp); repeat     |
| `* #`           | search for the word under the cursor           |
| `v V`           | select by character, by line                   |
| Enter           | make a card of the word, or of the selection   |
| `:>cmd`         | in `v` or `V`: pipe the selection to `cmd`     |
| `m`, `'`        | bookmark this line, go to the bookmark         |
| `e`             | show or hide English definitions               |
| `x`             | show or hide quotations                        |
| `c`, `E`        | show or hide MoEDict, Wiktionary as well       |
| `J K`           | scroll the definitions down, up                |
| Tab, Shift-Tab  | move into the definitions; back up a level     |
| `t`             | show or hide the other side's reading          |
| `gh`, F1        | show the keys                                  |
| Esc             | hide them; end a selection; back to the book   |
| `q`             | quit                                           |
| click, wheel    | move the cursor there; scroll the text         |

Tab moves the cursor into the definitions pane, which becomes the text: every
key above works on it as on the book, making cards and piping included, and a
pane below it defines the word under the cursor there. The cursor goes only
over the definitions, passing over the words, readings, sense numbers (1., (1),
①) and notes on them, as it passes over the number a numbered paragraph of the
book starts with. `j` and `k` keep its column on the screen, so they go
straight down a pane whose first line starts further left than the rest. The
pane below keeps half the screen however little it shows, so nothing above it
moves as the cursor goes from word to word. Tab again goes a level deeper. The
levels stack down the screen, each as tall as it was as a pane, and once they
no longer fit, the oldest are cut off at the top. Each level above marks in
blue the word it looked up. Shift-Tab goes back up a level, closing the pane it
leaves, and Esc goes back to the book from however deep. A click in the pane
moves into it, and one in a level above goes back up to it. A card made in a
pane takes the sense it is in as its context, and gets the book's line and the
words looked up as its source, as `/home/me/book.txt:12 › 讨论 › 交換`.
Bookmarks are for the book only.

Counts work as in vi (`5j`, `3w`). `:>cmd` starts `cmd` with sh, the
selected text on its standard input, and goes straight back to reading:
it is meant for commands that open a window of their own, such as
`:>st -e multizhdict` or `:>xclip -sel c`. The command runs in a session
of its own, so it outlives zhread, and its output is discarded; if it
fails, the end of what it wrote to stderr is shown in the status line. A
search in visual mode moves the end of the selection, as in vi.

The bookmark is one per file: setting it replaces the old one, and
opening the file goes to it. Bookmarks are kept
in `$XDG_STATE_HOME/zhread/bookmarks` (`~/.local/state` by default), with
the start of the marked line, so the mark finds its line again after the
file is edited above it.

### Making a card

Press Enter on the word, or select it with `v` and press Enter. A selection
that no dictionary has as one word, such as a phrase, is split by the
segmenter into the words in it that have definitions, and each gets a card of
its own, in turn, each with the context around it; `n` skips one and Esc
cancels the rest.

When the words can be divided more than one way, a screen asks how first:
牟取暴利 is one word to CC-CEDICT, and also 牟取 and 暴利. It offers the
word, or for a phrase the segmenter's words, then the other ways to divide it
wholly into words of two characters or more (words of one character are left
out, or every 散发 would offer 散 and 发), and for a phrase what the forward and
backward maximum-matching segmenters make of it. `j`/`k` and Enter, or a
digit, choose; Esc cancels.

The preview shows:

- **The context**, in both scripts when the word differs between them, else in
  the text's own, the cards then being made only in that script. It starts as
  the sentence holding the word: the text between sentence-ending marks (。！？…),
  with any closing quotes or brackets after them. A sentence under 12
  characters (「好。」) gets the sentence before it, or after it at the start of a
  paragraph; one over 60 is cut to the clauses nearest the word, at ，；：、. `[`
  and `]` move the left edge a clause left or right, `{` and `}` the right
  edge. A context that stops short of its sentence's end leaves out the comma
  or other clause mark it stops at. Lines not separated by a blank line are
  one paragraph, so a sentence hard-wrapped across lines is found whole.

  `k` from the first definition goes up onto the context, in the list like the
  definitions. `i` starts vi's editor on it, in normal mode: `h l w b e 0 ^ $`
  move (a run of Chinese characters is one word, as in vi, so `dw` deletes to
  the next punctuation), `x X r s D C`, and `d` or `c` with a motion (`dw`,
  `cb`, `d$`, `dd`) change, `i a I A` insert until Esc, and `u` undoes. Esc in
  normal mode goes back to the list, as do `j` and `k`, which move on as well;
  Enter saves. The bottom line starts with the mode: `-- LIST --`, `-- NORMAL
  --` or `-- INSERT --`; after it come only the card's questions and warnings,
  and at its right, as on the reader's rule, which of `x` (a ticked definition
  has quotations), `c` and `E` would change the card. `e` never would: the
  card lists the English definitions whatever it says. The keys are listed
  instead by `gh` or F1, as in the reader, in a pane below the card that `J`
  and `K` scroll. Esc in the list hides the keys before it cancels. Editing the text's own script converts the other script's
  line again, unless that line has been edited itself. The edge keys start
  again from the text, undoing the edits.
- **The definitions** shown in the pane when the card was begun (so MoEDict's
  and Wiktionary's beside the main ones only after `c` or `E`), Chinese first,
  each sense an option of its own, with its examples, so a card carries the
  sense the context uses rather than all of 打's. The list scrolls to keep the
  cursor in view. `j`/`k` move and Space ticks the ones that fit the context;
  the first Chinese and first English are ticked to start with. `c` and `E`
  show or hide MoEDict's and Wiktionary's definitions here too, keeping what
  is ticked. A ticked
  sense's quotations go on the card if `x` is showing them when it is saved,
  and are shown under it in the preview. The reading comes from the first
  ticked one.
- **The word's other contexts**: every other place in the book that has the
  word, unless the segmenter splits it between two words there, as 才能 in 刚才｜能吃,
  or, for a word of one character, makes it part of a longer one, as 打 of 打算 (a
  word of more inside a longer one, as 采访 in 采访记者, is nearly always that word
  compounded, and is kept). A doubled form of the word, AABB or ABAB for a word
  AB (松松垮垮, 研究研究) and AA, A一A or A了A for a word A (看看, 看一看, 看了看), is taken
  whole, so the card marks and blanks all of it rather than the word in its
  middle; so is one in the card's own context, though the card's word stays the
  one selected. Each comes with the context the card's own would start as, in
  the text's script. A context that shares text with the card's or with one
  before it is left out, as is one the same as another, so a sentence using the
  word twice is offered once, or not at all when it is the card's. All are
  ticked to start with; `j` goes on down to them from the definitions and Space
  unticks. `a` ticks them all, or unticks them all when they all are, so that
  from many the few wanted can be picked out. `[ ] { }` move the edges of the
  one under the cursor, and `i` edits it as the card's own context is edited.
  Its other script follows, converted again. The ticked ones go on the card
  with its own, and Anki shows them in turn (see below). A card made in a
  definitions pane gets the book's.
- **Cards already made** for the same word, or for a word containing it or
  contained in it, in either script, with their context. When there are
  any, Enter asks again before saving.

Enter saves, Esc cancels. An Esc right after another, as from insert mode out
past the list, asks first when the card has been changed (a context edited or
its edges moved, or a tick changed), and only `y` then throws the card away.
The other script's context comes from OpenCC's s2tw or tw2s, chosen by which
changes the paragraph more (see dict/README for why OpenCC). When the word is
the same in both scripts, the traditional fields are left empty and only the
simplified cards are made; for a traditional text it is the simplified contexts
that are left empty, and only the traditional cards are made. The source is the
text's full path and the line, so a card can be traced back to its book from
anywhere; it is kept in cards.tsv but not shown on the card. The reading is
followed by its zhuyin, "zǔ hé ㄗㄨˇ ㄏㄜˊ".

### The card file

Cards are appended to cards.tsv in the repository root (`-cards` to
change). It is the record of what has been carded and the file Anki imports.
Commit it: it is how the record survives.

### Into Anki

Once, import anki/zhread.apkg (File > Import, or open it). It creates the
zhread note type: the thirteen fields, the four card types and the styling
from anki/. It holds one placeholder note, which has no cards and so never
comes up in study; Anki imports a note type only along with a note that
uses it, so a package without one would create nothing.

After that, import cards.tsv with File > Import whenever there are new cards.
Its first lines are Anki's import headers, naming the note type, the deck
(`main`, or `-deck`) and the GUID column, so the import dialog needs no
settings. The GUID is a hash of the word and its context, so importing the
whole file again updates the notes Anki already has and adds the new ones,
without duplicates and keeping review history. An update overwrites the note's
fields, so fix a card in cards.tsv rather than in Anki, or the next import
undoes the fix.

After changing a template in anki/, run `make apkg` (the first run makes a
Python venv in .venv with genanki) and import anki/zhread.apkg again: the
note type keeps a fixed ID, so Anki updates it in place. A change to the
fields or to the card types or their order changes the note type's schema,
and Anki then imports it as a second note type, unless "Merge note types" is
ticked in the import dialog. Contexts and ContextsTrad were such a change,
added as the last two fields after the first cards were made: the cards
from before them have the two columns of cards.tsv empty.

Each time a card comes up, it shows the next of its contexts: the one made
with it, then the others ticked in the preview, in the order of the book,
held in the Contexts and ContextsTrad fields as one div each. The front
stores which it showed in the webview's localStorage, keyed by card type and
word, and the back shows the same one. Where that storage does not last
from one session to the next, the turn starts at a random context. A cloze
card blanks the other contexts as its own is blanked, in the card's script.

The recognition card shows the context with the word in bold, and asks for
the reading and meaning. The cloze card shows the context with the word
blanked and the definitions below, and asks for the word; the definitions
have the word blanked too, since MoE's examples often quote it. When a note
has cards in both scripts, the cloze blank says which it asks for, [简体] or
[繁體]. English definitions sit behind a fold on both. The 繁 cards are made
only for notes with a Traditional field, that is, where the two scripts
differ, and come first in the note type, so Anki introduces a note's 繁 cards
before its 简 ones.

The answer gives the word as the pane does, 組合（组～）, then the reading
and zhuyin and the definitions. Sizes are those of the cards from before
zhread (anki/style.css): the text 40px, the context 60px, the word 80px. The
traditional cards are in the Ministry of Education's standard Kai,
TW-MOE-Std-Kai, as those were, falling back to a serif font where it is not
installed, and the simplified ones in a serif font (Source Han Serif or Noto
Serif CJK where installed). The text is all one colour but for the word in the
context and the cloze blank, which are the orange of the reader's headwords.

`go run scripts/update_cards.go cards.tsv` brought the cards made before
these last changes up to date: the zhuyin, the clause mark at a cut context's
end, the cloze blanks' scripts. It keeps the GUIDs, so the import updates the
notes.
